A||N||5||Daleford||500 cu. in. Eldorado/Stretched Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||Daleford||12-06-2000||09:50 PM||||What are the negatives on installing a 500 cu. in. Eldorado drivetrain in a 7 in. stretched Fiero?  How is handling and cornering affected?      Thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-06-2000||10:19 PM||||Logitudinal installation or axial installation?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Daleford||12-06-2000||10:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Old Lar:<BR><B>Logitudinal installation or axial installation?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Longitudinal, as it was in the Caddy, using Caddy transaxel adapted to the Fiero axels<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||12-07-2000||03:08 PM||||The biggest disadvantage of the longitudinal caddy swap is weight increase. Much of the added weight will be behind the rear axle as well which creates even more handling problems. Other disavantages include the fact that there is no manual shift option, and there is not much available to hop-up the caddy engine. Here's a link to my website. Check out the caddywaggon link at the bottom too. <A HREF="http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fortunecity.com/silverstone/impala/403/longv8.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Daleford||12-07-2000||05:21 PM||||Had read your great site before Rockcrawl, but re-read it--very informative.  Am I much better off weight wise in going the same route but using the 350 olds?<BR>Also I always understood you can't weld a solid axel.  Does your Fiero axel attached to flangeplate stand up? thanks<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||12-07-2000||05:46 PM||||I really don't know the weight difference between all these engines. I used a Chevy 350, and it was pretty tail heavy. It wasn't terrible, but I could tell there was some extra weight there. I never had any problems with the axles. I welded the adapter plate to the tri-pot housing both inside and out after they were pressed together. This is how I've always seen them done, but maybe the Caddi tri-pot could be used with custom made axles. Of course this was in the lambo and I didn't drive it very hard (usually  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ). The guy who owns it now has not had any complaints, and he's a Vette guy.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
